  "id": "DrugBank_DB15404 - MONDO_5279",
  "verbalization": "Heparin, Bovine, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Pulmonary Embolism (disease) through a series of interconnected indications. The evidence suggests that Heparin is associated with Purpura Fulminans and Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation, both of which are conditions that can lead to thromboembolic events, including Pulmonary Embolism. Adomiparin, a drug linked to these conditions, is also associated with Pulmonary Embolism, indicating a potential mechanistic link between Heparin and the disease. The presence of multiple pathways involving Heparin and Adomiparin, along with the shared indication of Purpura Fulminans and Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation, supports a hypothesis that Heparin may contribute to the development of Pulmonary Embolism through mechanisms involving coagulation and thrombosis. The integrated evidence suggests that Heparin, through its role in coagulation and thrombosis, may be a potential candidate for repurposing in the treatment of Pulmonary Embolism, though further mechanistic validation is required to confirm this relationship.",
